The map $\mu\mapsto\{\hat{\mu}(n)\}$ is a continuous injective algebra homomorphism from $M(\mathbb{T})$ into $\ell^\infty(\mathbb{Z})$: (i) $\widehat{\mu\ast
u}(n)=\hat{\mu}(n)\hat{
u}(n)$. (ii) $\widehat{\mu+
u}(n)=\hat{\mu}(n)+\hat{
u}(n)$. (iii) $\widehat{\alpha\mu}(n)=\alpha\hat{\mu}(n)$. (iv) $\sup_n|\hat{\mu}(n)|\le\|\mu\|_1$. (v) If $\hat{\mu}(n)=0$ for all $n$, then $\mu=0$ (injectivity, by density of $\Pi(\mathbb{T})$ in $C(\mathbb{T})$). (vi) For $f\in L^1(\mathbb{T})$, $(\hat{f}(n))\in c_0(\mathbb{Z})$ (Riemann-Lebesgue lemma).
